Relapsing polychondritis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Auricular chondritis, Sparing of earlobe, Saddle-nose deformity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-039
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of intense, agonizing pain and swelling of the external ears, combined with severe anterior chest pain and new-onset hoarseness, requiring immediate evaluation to rule out acute airway compromise from tracheal inflammation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient with sudden, painful swelling of the ears and nose, now presenting with a hoarse voice and chest pain. Urgent after-hours bedside assessment was absolutely critical to evaluate the patency of the airway (laryngotracheal involvement), differentiate the chest pain from an acute myocardial infarction, and instigate immediate high-dose corticosteroids. Delaying this assessment until standard hours carried an imminent, life-threatening risk of sudden asphyxiation due to tracheal cartilage collapse.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Both of my ears are burning and incredibly swollen, my nose hurts to touch, and suddenly my voice is gravelly and my chest aches when I breathe."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 24-hour history of the sudden onset of intensely red, swollen, and tender auricular cartilage on both sides. The patient clearly notes the complete sparing of the non-cartilaginous earlobes. Over the past 12 hours, the patient developed severe pain over the bridge of the nose, with mild swelling (nasal cartilage inflammation). Critically, the patient reports new-onset laryngotracheal involvement causing hoarseness and a mild, high-pitched wheeze when breathing in (stridor), along with a feeling of tightness in the throat. Additionally complains of severe, sharp anterior chest wall pain when taking a deep breath (costochondritis). Reports a bilateral, non-erosive oligoarthritis in the wrists. Denies current eye pain or hearing loss.
Functional Status: Highly anxious, sitting rigidly upright, speaking in short sentences to avoid stressing the airway.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme risk of sudden, fatal airway collapse; spouse is terrified by the stridor.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 37.9 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is acutely distressed, tachypneic, and exhibits a notably hoarse, raspy voice. Mild inspiratory stridor is audible at rest.
Physical Exam: HEENT: Both auricles are violaceous, grossly swollen, and exquisitely tender to the lightest touch. The earlobes are distinctly pale, soft, and completely spared. The nasal bridge is swollen, erythematous, and highly tender, raising suspicion for impending saddle-nose deformity. Respiratory: Mild inspiratory stridor is present. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. The chest wall is exquisitely tender to palpation over the costochondral junctions bilaterally, mimicking pleuritic pain. Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, heart sounds dual, no murmurs. Diagnostic tetrad heavily supported clinically: profound auricular chondritis with classic sparing of the earlobe, and early signs of nasal cartilage destruction, anticipating a marked elevation of acute phase reactants (ESR/CRP) on hospital blood work.
Environmental Observations: Patient is sipping cold water constantly to soothe the throat.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and antihistamines taken with zero effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Relapsing polychondritis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe flare with impending airway compromise)
Differentials:

Acute Auricular Cellulitis/Perichondritis (Infectious) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly bilateral presentation, the concurrent severe inflammation of the nasal and costal cartilage, and the critical presence of laryngotracheal symptoms, defining a systemic autoimmune process rather than a localized skin infection.

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of generalized urticaria, the very specific localization of swelling strictly to cartilaginous structures (sparing the fleshy earlobe), and the more subacute (24hr) onset rather than a sudden histamine release.

Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (Wegener's) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While it causes saddle-nose deformity, GPA does not typically cause the explosive, painful auricular chondritis that is pathognomonic for RP.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Imminent threat to airway)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an archetypal, life-threatening flare of Relapsing Polychondritis, characterized by explosive inflammation of multiple cartilaginous structures (ears, nose, ribs, and most dangerously, the trachea). The presence of hoarseness and inspiratory stridor indicates acute laryngotracheal chondritis, placing the patient at imminent risk of complete airway collapse or severe glottic edema. Urgent after-hours assessment was absolutely mandated to physically triage the airway, identify the stridor, and trigger an immediate emergency 000 ambulance transfer. Deferring care until standard daytime hours would likely result in fatal asphyxiation, unequivocally justifying the MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The patient is in impending airway failure and requires immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 ambulance. The hospital must urgently secure the airway, consult ENT for an urgent flexible nasendoscopy, and initiate immediate, life-saving high-dose intravenous pulse corticosteroids (e.g., methylprednisolone) to halt the acute cartilaginous inflammation and prevent structural collapse of the trachea.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict airway positioning: Remain sitting bolt upright with the chin slightly elevated ("sniffing position"); explaining that this specific mechanical posture naturally splints the windpipe open, actively ensuring the maximum amount of air can pass the swollen vocal cords while waiting for paramedics. Voice rest: Absolutely zero speaking; communicating only by nodding or writing; linking this directly to resting the inflamed laryngeal cartilage, preventing further swelling and spasm of the airway.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None; critical care/ENT admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission expected.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched (Priority 1). If the patient begins to gasp for air, turns blue around the lips (cyanosis), or loses consciousness, the spouse must immediately update 000 and be prepared to perform CPR if breathing ceases entirely.

